WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.560
So you're about to start a family

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.130
or you've already started one.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.680
Having a child is a beautiful,
transcendent experience

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.887
but you're also gonna
find yourself asking,

00:00:15.887 --> 00:00:17.897
"How much is this going to cost me?"

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.913
I like him holding up this notebook.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.250
This episode is personal to me.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.820
Bobby here was born last year

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.100
and I often look at him

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.030
and I wonder what the future holds,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:39.880
not just for him

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.080
but for me and my wallet.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.070
What does daycare cost?

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.510
What do diapers cost?

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.140
Does he need a college fund?

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.690
It's pretty daunting to
add all that stuff up

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.490
but Cristina Livadary has
actually crunched the numbers.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.480
Cristina, thank you so much
for being on "Good Money".

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.653
I just had a baby, he's nine months old,

00:00:58.653 --> 00:01:01.850
so we're kind of going through this.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.670
There's figures out there
that most people get

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.090
about what having a kid costs.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.800
And you kind of delved into that

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.680
and saw that, saw what
the truth was, I suppose.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.170
It really does vary

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.010
and depend upon a number of factors.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.501
So a great cost that can vary a lot

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:26.100
is the cost of maternity healthcare.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.500
On average, Americans can expect to spend

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.870
if they're covered by
a health plan, $4,500

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.050
for routine maternity healthcare.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.820
That doesn't include, you know,

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.320
if you have an emergency C-section

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.320
or if you have a longer than
expected stay in the NICU.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.570
So $4,500 is really just
the base starting point.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.670
The next thing we wanted
to investigate was

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.280
what we call "getting
ready for baby" costs.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.220
How much are we going to spend

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.630
on getting that nursery ready?

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.830
Most of our clients spend about $5,000

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.840
for some of those bigger items

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.280
that don't get bought
during the baby shower.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:11.280
So that could be your crib,
your bassinet, your stroller.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.480
It's worth mentioning,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.790
the Baby Care Products market
is projected to be worth

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.283
over $100 billion worldwide by 2025.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.550
You could do parental or birthing classes

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.450
so that's like $650.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.700
That's, like, on average
for one of those classes

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.760
but there's also doulas
and lactation consultants.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.140
On average is about $2,500 for either

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.590
that lactation consultant and/or doula.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.340
So then the final part, the
most you'll spend on your child,

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.350
is childcare.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.360
The cost of childcare is enormous.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.240
Very early on, if you decide

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.010
and you have the means
to do a night nurse,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.650
that night nurse in LA can range anywhere

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.240
from $30 to $40 an hour.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.360
And then you have just basic childcare

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.530
so whether it's infant daycare, a nanny

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.610
and especially popular here in Los Angeles

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.630
is having an au pair

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.195
and so an au pair will
cost you $20,000 a year.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.810
But the other thing is that

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.670
they'll also have to live with you.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:19.930
So if you add all those up,

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.550
it comes to a grand total of $47,000.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.150
Ooh, yeah.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:25.983
Gosh.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.890
That's the cost of having a baby right now

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:31.320
but what about the future?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.730
Are they going to go to
college? Trade school?

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.670
When are they going to move
out of the house? Ever?

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.540
Roger Ma's a Certified Financial Planner

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.550
and he's been thinking
about this stuff a lot

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.580
especially since he has a
baby of his own named, Owen.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.483
Both of them joined us to talk.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:51.840
Let me just start by saying,
I haven't done anything

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.430
to prepare for Bobby's financial future.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:55.820
I'm starting at zero.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.820
What can I do today to
make that a rosier picture?

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.210
It's always good to get a sense

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:03.420
of where you're starting from.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.710
So I think

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.750
coming up with your financial report card

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.070
which includes calculating your net worth,

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.590
figuring out what your
living expenses are,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.500
how much you spend on a monthly basis.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.080
Yeah, Owen.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.700
And in what categories

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.220
and then also what your
credit score is as well

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.320
and I think that's a great starting point.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.690
Is saving for college
as big of a deal now,

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.460
as it used to be?

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.170
The cost of education has been increasing

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.960
substantially year after year

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.320
but honestly, I don't think we really know

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.940
what the, what form higher education

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.060
is gonna take in 15, 20 years.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.650
And if it's gonna be as important in terms

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.620
of employers using it for signaling

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.940
so I certainly recommend
to save within a 529 plan.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.970
A 529 plan is a tax
advantage savings account

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.300
designed to be used for a
beneficiary's education expenses,

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.203
like Bobby's.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.840
Let's say I do a 529 plan

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.340
and he grows up

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.150
and he's like, "I don't
want to go to college."

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.150
What happens to that money?

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.570
Yeah, so usually if you are taking it out

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.080
for non-eligible expenses,

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.563
you might get taxed at
your federal tax rate,

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:27.090
state tax, it might be
subject to local tax too

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.330
and then you might pay a penalty as well.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.480
That said, I think it's also
important to save within,

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.440
say a Roth IRA or a
taxable brokerage account.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.390
Money in those accounts
is a little more flexible

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.310
than money in a 529 plan.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.570
Are education expenses...

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:46.550
What does that include?

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.560
So I believe it includes
room, board, tuition,

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.660
certain electronic expenses, like computer

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.500
and some other technology.
Uh-huh.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.990
There's some flexibility
to change beneficiary

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.870
on the account so certainly
you might intend to use it

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.920
for Bobby's educational expenses

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.420
but let's say he goes to college,

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.860
you still have money left

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.390
and you want to take
an a class for yourself

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.760
and it's at an eligible institution.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.740
You could change the
beneficiary to yourself.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.620
I think the other two things,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.780
that would be helpful
to get into place are

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.490
a term life insurance policy

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.820
outside of your employer coverage

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.280
and then making sure that you
have an estate plan in place.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.510
And if you already have one in place,

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.270
updating it to incorporate your little one

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.053
and particularly, not only,

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.890
figuring out where your stuff goes

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.410
but in the case where you're not around

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.770
and your partner isn't around,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.190
who is going to take care of Bobby?

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.590
So start a 529 plan,

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.450
get a term life insurance
policy outside of my employer

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.033
and change my estate plan
or will to include Bobby.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.040
All right, so I'm starting to feel better

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.230
now that I know the cost,

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:04.930
it's probably going to be in the tens

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.580
of thousands of dollars.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.750
This is information that
I wish I'd had a while ago

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:13.713
and that I hope that
he has someday as well.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:16.840
Unfortunately, he's probably
not going to learn this

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.840
in our traditional school system.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.313
The best example's gonna
be learning from you.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.520
How does one teach kids

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:26.820
to have a good relationship with money?

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.340
Even if you don't feel like

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.080
you have a great relationship with money,

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:33.860
you have a good understanding
of your own money situation.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.830
Sharing your money ideas

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:40.040
and any experiences with money.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.540
Money is in every facet of our life,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.020
going to the grocery
store, driving our cars,

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.520
living in our houses.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.610
So being open and honest
with our children,

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.260
when you're making a grocery list

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:52.220
and going and looking at the ads

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.100
to see what's on sale this week.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.550
When you're talking about
doing chores in the house,

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.060
if you're going to give an allowance.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.610
And why we would be doing
that for our children,

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.820
like, "Why we're giving
you the allowance."

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.360
And just leading by example

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.300
and not being afraid of your
own insecurities around money

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.450
because those are going to
transpose onto your children.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:14.300
What would you suggest
is like a proper amount

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.750
to divulge about, you know,

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.440
income,

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.400
comfort?

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.940
Just being more open,

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.690
even if it's like
possibly negative things.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.670
Telling your kids, "We don't
have enough money for that."

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:27.590
Or, "Remember we want to go

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.750
and take this vacation next month

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.030
so this month we're not
going to go out to dinner

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.340
or we're not going to
go to Chuck E. Cheese

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.180
or to the movies,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.470
we're gonna try to save our money

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.160
because we want to have money for next,

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:41.790
our next trip or next month."

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.100
And just explaining that
process to children,

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.670
what's actually going on in your head,

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:47.820
I think is very helpful

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.860
because a lot of times they don't see it.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.200
They don't understand how
the money even comes in,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.490
why daddy goes to work,

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:55.833
why mommy goes to work,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.180
that putting gas in the car costs money,

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.170
sometimes they don't even
see those transactions.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.250
So allowing them to see those transactions

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.743
is always very helpful.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:11.070
So we talked about expenses, planning

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.120
and teaching your kids about money.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.320
Now, I hope that the school system starts

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.700
to make financial education
a little bit more commonplace

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.190
but until then,

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.390
we're going to have to
just do it ourselves.

